==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|de|Elend||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|eln}}, {{m|de|elent}} (mid-13th c.). Usally derived from {{der|de|olt|elenis}} (“deer”, modern {{m|lt|élnias}}), from {{der|de|ine-pro|*h₁el-}}. This derivation is supported by the fact that the {{m|gmh||el(e)n}}-form appears during a time when the elk was gradually dying out in the German-speaking lands. However, it must be noted that the Lithuanian word does not refer to the elk specifically. Even more importantly, {{m|gmh||elen}} (from {{m|gmh|elhen}} by regular loss of prevocalic ''h'' in [[Central German|Central]] and [[Low German]] dialects) can represent a generalised [[oblique]] case or plural of {{der|de|gem-pro||*elhô}}, weak-stem variant of {{m|gem-pro|*elhaz}}. This would make it a mere byform of {{doublet|de|Elch|notext=1}}.<ref>{{R:nl:EWN|eland}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈeːlən/}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|n:m}}

# {{lb|de|archaic}} [[moose]], Eurasian [[elk]] {{gloss|''[[Alces alces]]''}}
#: {{syn|de|Elch}}

